From 2b16bffd237d2949aab3763b5181325c0b775f7b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Payton <brandon@happycode.net>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 21:11:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 11/22] Go: Port Kandelo runtime to upstream wasm32
 architecture

Forward-port Kandelo OS, syscall, shared-memory atomics, scheduler and
linker support from 5834adc onto the upstream wasm32 review stack at
5e196a61. This prototype enables kandelo/wasm32 with four-byte Go pointers
and integers, retaining upstream eight-byte registers and continuations.

Keep logical PCs separate from function table handles in clone startup,
signals and C data relocations. Adapt atomic ABI0 frames and uintptr
operations to the target width. Follow Go 1.28 lock accounting, scavenger
zeroing and directory-stat contracts. Retain upstream WASI and js targets.

Omit the old regexp algorithm change and mixed-width cgo field/frame
adaptations. The C debug reader remains, with ordinary typed pointer
fields and a homogeneous frame layout. Cgo execution is not yet ported;
this checkpoint enables plain Go only and does not change package pins.

Validation: full std builds for kandelo/wasm32, wasip1/wasm32,
wasip1/wasm and js/wasm; cmd/cgo, Wasm object-reader and generated platform
tests; eight functional checks on both WASI variants. Kandelo Node and
Chromium/Firefox/WebKit pass pointer layout, collector root retention,
continuations, concurrent locked Ms, shared atomics and VFS/startup probes.
These bounded checks do not establish full Go conformance or cgo support.

